## 2. Writable Precursor Nanoparticle Sheets
Easily writable ceramic precursor sheets were prepared via 3D self-assembly of metal oxide nanoparticles from scalable flame-made hot aerosols (Figure 1a, Figure S1, Supporting Information).[28] Following the cluster-cluster aggregation mechanism in the diffusion-limited regime, the resulting ceramic precursor layers consist of a highly-fragile morphology of metal oxide nanoparticles loosely bonded by van der Waals forces and a porosity of ca. \(98\%^{[28\mathrm{a},29]}\) (Figure S2a, Supporting Information). A small \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) average particle size of less than \(20\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S3, Supporting Information) was synthesized to facilitate the full reaction of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticles to ZIF-8 via gas-phase conversion.[13,30]
The thickness of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor sheet was controlled by the aerosol deposition time and arbitrarily fixed to \(68~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) to explore the conversion of relatively thick nanoparticle layers into thick high-aspect-ratio MOF patterns (Figure S2, Supporting Information). The thickness of this precursor nanoparticle layer, and thus resulting MOF films, can be tuned in the \(80\,\textrm{n m}\) to \(230~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) range via controlling the nanoparticle aerosol self-assembly time (Figure S4, Supporting Information).[31] Importantly, while we have focused on the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) to ZIF-8 system as a conceptual proof of our patterning approach, the flame synthesis route affords the preparation of a large range of metal oxide nanoparticle aerosols, including CuO and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) , that could be used as precursors for HKUST-1 and MIL\(53(\mathrm{Fe})\) , respectively.[31] To be noted, these loosely bonded NNs, hold together by weak van der Waals forces, enables facile patterning from the nano- up to the centimeter-scale by a variety of approaches including ion beam, laser, and handheld writing with a pen. Artless handwritings were also obtained by using a pen in a similar fashion to writing on a paper (Figure 1c–e and Figure S5, Supporting Information). Furthermore, an intricate "Rose" (Figure 1f–j) approaching nanometer-scale resolution was demonstrated by focused ion beam (FIB) among others.
The micrometer-scale patterning of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor sheets (Figure 1b, Figure S6, Supporting Information) was conducted using a computer-assisted programmable micromachining laser ablation system (JPSA IX-100). Thanks to the fragile nature of the nanoparticle precursor layers, upon a short-timed laser irradiation, the exposed layer surface is removed via Coulomb Explosion, in line with the intense electromagnetic fields of the laser.[32] This enabled a rapid deep lithography of the nanoparticle precursor films. Notably, an exemplary set of complex \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor patterns, having a depth of \(68~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) , were obtained within less than \(30~\mathrm{s}\) over a geometrical surface of \(4\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) (Figure 2a). By testing the same laser ablation protocol on dense \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) films, a maximum thickness of \(0.8~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) could be used for the fabrication
of patterns; this highlights the important contribution of the porous ZnO NN for the fabrication of thick patterns.
The laser processing speed and loop numbers (Supporting Information Laser parameters and descriptions of different patterns) were adjusted to the required amount of ZnO to be removed for each pattern and the required resolution, while keeping a constant laser fluence of ca. \(0.18~\mathrm{J~cm}^{-2}\). For instance, low-resolution patterns such as capital letters (Figures 1b and 2a) were written at the relatively high processing speed of
\(15~\mathrm{mm~s}^{-1}\) in 5 processing loops. However, when a higher resolution is required (i.e., all the other patterns having different geometrical shapes) the laser writing speed was set at \(5~\mathrm{mm~s}^{-1}\). In Figure 2a, lines were obtained within 4 writing loops, while the disk arrays were written with 10 loops because of both contributions 1) the curvature required for the disks and 2) the larger amount of ZnO to be removed. The number of loops was increased to 15 for the circles, triangles, and rectangles following the increasing amount of ZnO to be removed.
Overall, these fragile nanoparticle precursor sheets provide an easily laser-writable material for the rapid fabrication of deep ceramic patterns with standard micro-machining laser systems
without the need for laborious infrastructure requirements such as those for removal of heat generated by high power laser ablation of dense ceramic films.[33]
Comparing to conventional compact thin-film precursors, made of dense ceramic layers, the energy required to pattern our NN precursor sheets is much lower. In details, given the identical laser settings (e.g., input power, beam size, wavelength, current, etc.) and geometry design, we were able to achieve \(68~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) deep patterns in the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) NN precursor, while dense \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) films (i.e., sputtered \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) thin film), we could only pattern \(800\,\mathrm{nm}\) depth. This allows for the facile and rapid mask-less patterning of very thick \((\upmu\mathrm{m}\cdot\mathrm{mm})\) MOF layers that are required for various applications, such as microfluidic channels and enzymatic reactors.
## 3. High-Fidelity Conversion of Patterned MOFs Monoliths
Monolithic MOF films with high aspect-ratio patterns were obtained by vapor-phase conversion[13,31]of the laser-ablated precursor sheets (Figure 1b). Figure 2b shows a patterned ZIF-8 film with a surface of \(4~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) obtained by exposure of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor sheet to 2-MIM vapor at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Notably, the crosssectional SEM images of the MOF film in Figure 2 (Figure S2b, Supporting Information) reveal an in-depth MOF pattern of tens of micrometers. The ZIF-8 pattern dimensions could be easily scaled down by four times to a total surface of ca. \(1\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) without major loss of resolution (resolution of ca. \(70\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) or less) (Figure 2c). Notably, the resolution is mainly determined by the parameters (e.g., dimensions, output power, and wavelength) of the accessible light source, advanced lithography techniques such as X-Ray and electron beam would drastically enhance the resolution possibly down to nanometer-scale.[24]
Figure 2d shows two orthogonally intersecting channels with a width of \(24~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) written on the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor sheet. Upon conversion to ZIF-8 (Figure 2e), the patterned design was preserved and a broadening of the channels could be observed, resulting in two intersecting \(70~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) wide channels surrounded by a monolithic ZIF-8 film. Such channel broadening could only be observed for laser patterning at relatively large-scale (i.e., micrometer-scale in width). This might attribute to the localized sintering of few \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticles during laser processing. After gas phase MOF reaction, the shrinkage and densification of those regional networks consisting of sintered \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticles/clusters located at the patterned edges would result in the broadening of channels. Notably, the channel broadening effect becomes muted for nanoscale patterning thanks to the accurately-confined energy dispersion of FIB. Figure 2f shows two arrow-like intersecting channels written on the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) sheet with the same laser spot size of \(24~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) , and resulting in the same \(70\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) width intersections on ZIF-8 films. Importantly, all precursor pattern features, such as the sharp acute-angular \((52^{\circ})\) back-walls and obtuse-angular \((128^{\circ})\) frontwalls (Figure 2f,g), were well-retained without fracturing of the resulting monolithic ZIF-8 film. The precise transfer of the original ceramic nanoparticle pattern to the MOF is attributed to the localized reaction of the precursor nanoparticles with the 2-MIM ligand vapor.
The dense packing of the resulting ZIF-8 film grains was confirmed by morphological analysis over large regions of the film surface. Figure 2h–j shows higher magnification SEM
characterization of a negative pattern of capital letters "MOF" within a ZIF-8 film. Notably, a dense and homogenous film morphology is observed throughout the bulk, patterned area, and interface. The ZIF-8 grains reveal a uniform size distribution with a visible grain size of ca. \(400{-}550\ \mathrm{nm}\) . A sharp deep interface is observed at the pattern border with micrometer range roughness giving a highlighted appearance to the patterned area (Figure 2h). Few MOF grains are sitting within \(30\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) of the pattern wall on the substrate surface (Figure 2h–j), which are attributed to those \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticles sticking to the substrate surface during the conversion of the dense ZIF-8 film.
To demonstrate the ease of obtaining increasingly more sophisticated MOF pattern designs, various structures were written on the precursor sheets (Figure 2a) with arbitrarily tunable dimensions (e.g., 4 and \(1~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) ), and transferred into monolithic ZIF-8 patterns. The latter designs included the negative patterns of capital letters (Figure 2c, top left), \(4\times4\) array of disks with a diameter of 200 and \(100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) (Figure 2b,c, top right), rectangular impounding reservoir connected to a circular pool via three channels (Figure 2b, middle left), zig-zag channel (Figure 2b, middle right), intersecting circles with full and empty surfaces as well as triangles (Figure 2b, bottom left), long perpendicular intersecting channels (Figure 2b, bottom middle) as well as a serpentine channel (Figure 2b, bottom right). These experiments demonstrate the potential of the laser ablation protocol for the fabrication of thick patterns of pure ZIF-8 MOF with pre-determined shapes.
Figure 3 shows higher magnification of the \(24~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) deep rectangular "impounding reservoir" (Figure 3a–c) connected to three horizontal channels \((70~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) wide), the circular pool of \(480~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) in diameter connected to the serpentine channel \((370~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) wide) (Figure 3d–f), and the curved channel \((70~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) wide) written under a triangle (Figure 3g–i). The higher magnification edge-surface views of the above pattern borders (Figure 2i,j, Figure 3b,c,e,f,h,i) show no visible compositional difference from the bulk film surface, indicating that the laser-ablation writing of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticle precursor sheets has negligible impact on the MOF conversion process. The latter designs show feasibilities for the rapid patterning of deep (i.e., \(24~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) ) and complex curved MOF structures with micro-scale precision on surfaces of several centimeter squares.
To investigate the mechanical stability of our patterned MOF layers, a standard abrasion test (ASTM D5264–98) was adopted for over 1600 cycles with \(2.27\,\mathrm{kg}\) loading. (Figure S7, Supporting Information, the thickness, measured by confocal microscopy, of a representative ZIF-8 layer with an initial thickness of ca. \(8.5\pm0.90\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) before and after the abrasion cycles). After the first 800 cycles, the initial layer thickness is reduced from ca. \(8.5\pm0.90\) to ca. \(5.5\,\pm\,0.63~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) . This might attribute to the removal of a top layer, consisting of more loosely ZIF-8 particles converted from redeposited \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticle clusters during laser processing. Further abrasion test of up to 1600 cycles reveals a nearly unvaried thickness, showing that our patterned MOF is sufficiently robust for handling and possible utilization in microfluidic devices.
The complete conversion of the ceramic precursor sheet into a MOF film was confirmed by a complementary set of chemical and physical characterization approaches including XRD, FTIR,
XPS, HRTEM, and HAADF-STEM with elemental mappings, aiming at identifying possible contamination arising from the laser-ablation process (Figure 4a,b). The XRD analysis was carried out on the bare Si wafer used as substrate, pure flame-made \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) powders, patterned (flame-made) \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor sheet on the Si wafer substrate, the resulting patterned ZIF-8 monolithic film on the Si wafer substrate, and representative ZIF-8 crystals synthesized via an alternate aqueous system synthesis route.[34] The XRD characteristic peaks of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor sheet (Figure 4c, light blue line) reveal a wurtzite \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) crystal structure that is in line with the diffraction patterns of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticles (Figure 4c, dark blue line) collected from the flame-made aerosols with glass fiber filters. After reaction with the 2-MIM vapor, the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) wurtzite diffraction peaks of the precursor sheet were substituted by the diffraction peaks of the sodalite (sod) ZIF-8 crystal structure (Figure 4c, violet line). The latter matched well with those of the ZIF-8 crystals (Figure 4c, orange line), synthesized via the aqueous system route.[34] To further confirm the conversion of the patterned \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor sheet to ZIF-8, FTIR measurements were conducted on the same samples (Figure 4d). The characteristic stretching of \(Z\mathrm{n}{-}\mathrm{O}\) bond[28b] at \(440~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was observed for the patterned \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor sheet (Figure 4d, light blue line), in line with that of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) powders (Figure 4d, dark blue line). Upon reaction with the 2-MIM vapor, the characteristic \(Z\mathrm{n}{-}\mathrm{O}\) bond[31] of ZIF-8 at \(423\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (Figure 4d, violet line). Furthermore, the characteristic modes of ZIF-8 imidazole ring observed at 1500–1350, 1350–900, and \(800{-}600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (Figure 4d, violet line) were assigned to the ring stretching, in plane bending, and out of plane bending vibrational modes, respectively. All the FTIR modes were also in good agreement
with those observed with the ZIF-8 powders (Figure 4d, orange line), obtained via solution-based synthesis.[34] The combined XRD and FTIR analyses suggest a complete conversion of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) ceramic precursor to sod ZIF-8.
To further investigate the possible presence of minor impurities, XPS and TEM analyses were pursued. Two representative locations were determined for XPS point measurements on both the patterned \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor sheets and resulting ZIF-8 monolithic films (Figure S8a, Supporting Information). The latter consisted of a location sitting exactly on the edge of a patterned disk (Figure S8a, red point, location-i) and a location a few hundred micrometers distant from the pattern border (Figure S8a, Supporting Information, blue point, location-ii).
High-resolution Zn core level spectra before conversion at the pattern edge (Figure S8, Supporting Information) indicated a pure \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) surface chemistry (Figure S8b, Supporting Information) with a binding energy of 1021 and \(1044~\mathrm{eV}\) for \(Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}\,2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}\,2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) , respectively. While after conversion a mixed surface chemistry (Figure S8c, Supporting Information) was observed which is attributable to a major ZIF-8 component with some \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) contribution after conversion. To clarify, the \(Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}\,2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) spectra (Figure 4e, black line with red dots) of the laser-ablated surface (Figure S8a, Supporting Information, red point, location-i) after conversion can be fitted by two quasi-Gaussian components (Figure 4e, blue and purple line) peaking at 1018 and \(1021~\mathrm{eV}\) corresponding to the low energy \(Z\mathrm{n}{-}\mathrm{N}\) covalent bonding and higher energy \(Z\mathrm{n}{-}\mathrm{O}\) ionic bonding, respectively, indicating a mixed surface chemistry consisting of both ZIF-8 and \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) residuals. In stark contrast, the \(Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}\,2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) spectra of the location distant from the pattern edge (Figure 4e, violet line with blue dots at the bottom) after conversion
presented a pure ZIF-8 surface chemistry with a single component peaking at 1018 eV reflecting the covalent \(\mathrm{Zn-N}\) bonding
only, indicating the complete replacement of \(\mathrm{Zn-O}\) ionic bonding by \(\mathrm{Zn-N}\) covalent bonding.
HRTEM, HAADF-STEM, and elemental mappings were conducted on \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticles (Figure 4f–i) and ZIF-8 grains (Figure 4j–m) detached from the patterned \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor sheets and ZIF-8 films, respectively. The former revealed a pure \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticle composition (Figure 4f,g) with a uniform distribution of \(Z\mathrm{n}\) (Figure 4h) and O (Figure 4i). The latter grains revealed a pure ZIF-8 grain composition (Figure 4j) with a uniform distribution of \(Z\mathrm{n}\) (Figure 4k), N (Figure 4l), and C (Figure 4m) and without any observation of oxygen-containing \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) domains. The combined XPS and electron microscopy analysis indicate the conversion of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor into ZIF-8 with the presence of a small fractional content of \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) impurities localized at the pattern edge. The latter is possibly attributed to the sintering of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticles at the laser-exposed pattern edge. The extreme local temperature induced by laser[32,35] may result in the sintering growth and packing of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticles (Figure 4a). Particles growing above \(20\ \mathrm{nm}\) and packed clusters with porosity less than \(95\%^{[31]}\) may experience only partial conversion to ZIF-8 structures by gas phase conversion, due to the ligand reaction being limited within the first \(10\ \mathrm{nm}\) of the particle surface.[13,30] Due to the drastic reduction of laser energy within thin films,[35] the sintered area would possibly occur within a maximal penetration radius of \(100{-}112\ \mathrm{nm}^{[35]}\) from the laser-ablated area. Overall, given the limited fabrication methods of monolithic MOFs,[13,23,30,36] the feasibility of rapidly fabricating arbitrarily-designed MOF patterns with micrometer scale thickness has various potential applications including micro-fluidic[26] and renewable energy systems.[27]
## 4. MOFs Patterns of Micrometer-Scale Depth with Resolution Down to Nanometer-Scale
To further demonstrate the resolution range and flexibility of our approach, we performed further mask-less patterning of our \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticle sheets by high-resolution FIB and with a low-resolution hand-held pen. We demonstrate that our approach allows for paper-like writing/patterning of MOFs with any arbitrary geometry. Figure 1c,d shows the writing of "iMOF" by smooth and fluid strokes on our \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticle precursor sheets. After conversion, the "iMOF" pattern is well preserved with no loss of details (Figure 1e). This facile maskless patterning procedure has potential for the rapid patterning of very large MOF surfaces that do not require high resolutions including microfluidics,[26c,37] biosensors,[26a] and enzymatic reactors.[38]
To demonstrate high-resolution patterning with our approach, a "Rose" with a total dimension of ca. \(90\times60~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}^{2}\) was patterned on the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticle precursor sheet (Figure 1f,g) by FIB. Notably, this is an intricate pattern with a tiny line width of less than \(270\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 1g). After gas phase reaction, no distortion could be found on the patterned ZIF-8 layer (Figure 1h–j) and the total dimension of the "Rose" remains unchanged with no loss of original details. For the very tiny line of less than \(300\ \mathrm{nm}\) in width drawn on the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor sheet (Figure 1g), a slight increase of line width can be observed (i.e., Figure 1h–j, the resulting ZIF-8 is ca. \(400\ \mathrm{nm}\) ). This is attributed to the expansion of the lattice structure from
\(Z\mathrm{nO}\) to ZIF-8. In detail, our nanoparticle precursor sheets consist of \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticles with an average diameter of ca. \(14.8\ \mathrm{nm}\) , thus, the size of resulting ZIF-8 particles should be around \(250\ \mathrm{nm}\) , considering a 17-fold lattice expansion. This is rarely achieved among the most up-to-date advanced MOF patterning techniques,[7,8,21–24] and has never been achieved with deep MOF patterns of tens of micrometers. In light of these results, our patterning method presents a very high-resolution that is likely to be sufficient for most envisioned MOF applications.
With respect to the broader applicability of our method, it is important to note that our proposed approach is compatible with the broad family of CVD-generated MOFs.[13,23,24,31] As the flame aerosol synthesis approach, utilized here, is capable to synthesize a large family of metal oxides,[39] which makes it an ideal tool for synthesizing nanoparticle precursor sheets for various MOFs. To demonstrate this concept, we have now converted MIL-53 (Figures S10 and S11, Supporting Information) and HKUST-1 films (Figures S12 and S13, Supporting Information) from \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{CuO}\) NN precursor sheets, respectively. XRD results of both \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) (Figure S11, Supporting Information) and \(\mathrm{CuO}\) (Figure S13, Supporting Information) precursor sheets before and after conversion reveals the successful production of other MOF families than ZIFs via our proposed method. This further indicates the possible applications of our proposed patterning procedure across various MOF materials.
To summarize, our method enables i) both low and high-resolution patterning down to ca. \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\) , ii) is applicable to various MOF families and highly compatible with existing CVD-MOF growth, and iii) afford in-depth monolithic MOF patterns of up to several tens of micrometers in thickness.
## 5. Conclusion
In summary, we have demonstrated the facile fabrication of centimeter-scale monolithic MOF patterns with a depth of tens of micrometers and a broad range of resolution via various approaches including FIB, laser ablation, and handheld pen. Easily laser-writable nanoparticle sheets, consisting of loosely connected NNs of less than \(20\ \mathrm{nm}\) in diameter and a porosity of \(98\%\) , were introduced as a large-area precursor for the design and fabrication of deep and arbitrarily complex MOF patterns. Gas-phase conversion of patterned \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) nanoparticle sheets, resulted in monolithic MOF films, with designable dimensions and thickness over surfaces of several square centimeters. A range of representative ZIF-8 patterns was demonstrated including \(24\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\)-deep capital letters, circular disk arrays, interconnected impounding reservoir, geometric drawings, superimposed zig-zag, perpendicular and serpentine channels. Notably, artless handwritings were also patterned on ZIF-8 film using a handheld pen. A vastly pure MOF composition of the converted patterns was confirmed by a series of physical and chemical characterizations with a small fractional content of ceramic impurity localized at the laser-ablated pattern edge. This nanoparticle-precursor sheet approach provides a promising route for the engineering of functional MOF
patterns and their integration in devices with envisioned applications in microfluidics, energy storage, and filtration.
## 6. Experimental Section
A liquid-fed flame reactor (Figure S1, Supporting Information) was utilized to generate the aerosol source consisting of ZnO nanoparticles with an optimal concentration for the ZnO NN precursor preparations as reported elsewhere.[28a] In detail, a mixed solution of zinc-naphthenate dissolved in m-xylene with a total metal concentration of \(0.3\;\mathsf{m o l}\;\mathsf{L}^{-1}\) was used as the metal precursor for the production of ZnO nanoparticles with an averaged particle size of \(18.6\;\mathsf{n m}\) (Figure S3, Supporting Information). All ZnO NN precursors were collected at \(20\;\mathsf{c m}\) height above the burner (HAB) resulting in a sufficiently high porosity of \(98\%\) .[28a,29] The thickness of ZnO NN precursor was arbitrarily kept as \(68~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) everywhere. The control ZnO powders were collected at the same HAB.
The laser ablation was carried out using a computer-assisted programmable micromachining system, namely JPSA IX-100, equipped with the PRISMA-532-14-V laser source. The original drawings of various patterns (Figure S6, Supporting Information) were designed via Microsoft Visio, which was a compatible software with the applied micromachining system. After being imported to the micromachining operation system, all dimensions were specified based on client requirements (e.g., the total dimensions of \(2.0\,\times\,1.9\,\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) and \(1.0~\times\) \(0.95\ \ c m^{2})\) . Varied laser powers \((200{-}300~\mathrm{~mV})\) , processing speeds \((5{-}15\ \mathsf{m m}\ \mathsf{s}^{-1})\) , and numbers of processing loops (5–10 loops) were customized according to different patterns.
The patterned monolithic ZIF-8 films were prepared via converting those patterned ZnO NN precursors in gas phase. In particular, the patterned ZnO NN precursor together with a glass vial containing 2-methylimidazole (2-MIM) solid crystals were placed in a Teflon lined stainless steel autoclave. The auto-clave was sealed and heated up to \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with a ramping of \(10\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\;\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}\) , and the reaction was held for \(18\;\mathfrak{h}\) . The control ZIF-8 powders were synthesized using a modified aqueous system based on a previous report.[34] In detail, two solutions were prepared separately in advance, including solution-1 of 8 mmol 2-MIM dissolved in 300 mmol water and solution-2 of 1 mmol zinc acetate dissolved in 300 mmol water. Then, solution-2 was added into solution-1 companied with magnetic stirring for \(2\,\,\mathrm{h}\) at room temperature. White powder was collected by centrifugation for \(10\ \mathsf{m i n}\) , washing twice with ethanol, and drying overnight in the oven \((60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) .
The XRD measurements were conducted using a Bruker system (XRD, D2 Phaser, USA) equipped with Cu Kα radiation of average wavelength 1.54059 Å. The FTIR data were collected using Bruker ALPHA II FTIR Spectrometer with the PLATINUM ATR module from 4000 to \(400~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) . The XPS point measurements were carried out and collected using a Thermofisher Kratos Axis Supra photoelectron spectrometer, with a monochromated Al kα source \((1486.7\,\mathsf{e V})\) at pass energy 20.
The SEM morphological characterizations were conducted using a Zeiss Ultraplus (FESEM) at \(3~\mathrm{kV}.\) The HRTEM, HAADF-STEM, and elemental mappings were collected using a JEOL 2100F FETEM at \(200~\mathrm{kV}.\)
FIB (FEI Helios 600 NanoLab) was used for the fine pattering of ZIF-8 films (Figure S9, Supporting Information).
A motorized device (Supporting Information, ZIF-8 cloth rub durability test, Figure S14 and Movie S1, Supporting Information) was applied for the cloth rub test.
A confocal microscope (SensoFar S Neox) was used as an optical profilometer to measure the changes in the thickness of MOF-coatings after the mechanical stability evaluation. The thickness measurements were based on the substrate surface which was masked during the film deposition (i.e., uncoated surface).
